<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roy Welensky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roy Welensky was a white Northern Rhodesian politician, born in Salisbury, who rose from railway worker and trade union leader to become prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> from 1956 to 1963. A leading advocate of the federation, he defended white political dominance while promising gradual partnership with Africans. He could not prevent the secession of Nyasaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> and the federation's dissolution in 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roy-welensky.jpg" alt="Roy Welensky" width="206" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roy_welensky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
